{"agentDecisions":{"entries":[{"answeredBy":"agent","body":"**Question:** The low definition exists but reviewers rate unusual-combination\nfindings high because of their consequence. What makes the rating reliable?\n**Answer:** A likelihood-first rule in the reviewer instructions (code and\nplanning reviewers): judge whether the scenario is realistic before weighing\nits consequence. A scenario is realistic when it arises in ordinary use within\nthe handled conditions, with no one acting unusually. A scenario that needs\ntwo or more independent uncommon conditions at once (for example a crash or\nsignal at one specific step together with a concurrent writer, a hand-edited\nor corrupted record together with a retry, a clock change during a provider\noutage) is low whatever its consequence. A severe consequence never raises an\nunusual scenario above low. The instructions name the conditions to count, so\na reviewer can state them in the failure scenario.\n**Answered by:** agent\n**Options:** (a) instructions only, with a counting rule and examples (chosen);\n(b) a structured `conditions` field in the finding schema with a mechanical\ndowngrade when it lists two or more: rejected, because it changes the verdict\nschema #142 is editing tonight, and a mechanical downgrade changes the\nreviewer's meaning, which the correction policy forbids; (c) leave the D8 text\nas it is: rejected, the evidence shows it is not applied.\n**Reversibility:** high; instruction text, versioned through the review\ncontract version, so retained results keep their recorded qualification.\n**Decision:** (a).","file":"interview.md","heading":"D1. How severity calibration becomes reliable"},{"answeredBy":"agent","body":"**Question:** D11 says code reviewers receive the failure model. How?\n**Answer:** Reviewers already receive `requirements/spec.md` (or\n`requirements/brief.md` in a light run) whole. The instructions name its\n`Failure model` section as the stated failure model: a finding whose trigger\nlies outside the handled conditions is low; a finding that the refusal\nbehaviour itself fails (outside the model, GateReeve should detect and refuse,\nbut does a silent best effort) is judged on its scenario as usual, because the\nrefusal is in the model. When the requirement basis has no `Failure model`\nsection, no finding is low merely for lying outside a model (D14). Planning\nreviewers get the same rule and also judge whether the model has an obvious\ngap.\n**Answered by:** agent\n**Options:** (a) name the section in the instructions, no packet change\n(chosen); (b) extract the section into a `packet.failureModel` field:\nrejected, it edits reviewer packet assembly, which #142 owns tonight, and the\nreviewer already has the whole document.\n**Reversibility:** high.\n**Decision:** (a).","file":"interview.md","heading":"D2. Where reviewers get the failure model"},{"answeredBy":"agent","body":"**Question:** What exactly does `spec.validate` require of the section?\n**Answer:** A level-2 heading `## Failure model` (case-insensitive) outside\ncode fences, followed before the next level-1 or level-2 heading by at least\none line of content outside code fences that is not only whitespace. A\nsection whose content contains `TODO` is unresolved. Each failure names the\nline of the heading, or says the heading is missing.\n**Answered by:** agent\n**Options:** (a) level-2 heading, nonempty, no TODO (chosen; matches the\ntemplate and the existing `## Acceptance Criteria` convention); (b) also\nrequire the two parts (handled conditions and detect-and-refuse): rejected, it\nis a semantic judgment that planning review already makes, and wording varies.\n**Reversibility:** high.\n**Decision:** (a).","file":"interview.md","heading":"D3. Failure-model section check"},{"answeredBy":"agent","body":"**Question:** Which lines are linted, which words, and what satisfies the\nlint?\n**Answer:** The lint reads each line of the `## Acceptance Criteria` and\n`## Rubric` sections (and, for a spec in an alternative presentation without\nthose headings, each extracted AC and rubric line). It ignores fenced code and\ninline code spans. The words are D11's list, matched case-insensitively on\nword boundaries: `always`, `any`, `all cases`, `never fails`, `converges`,\n`robust`. A line passes when the same line also carries a bound: a reference\nto the failure model (the words \"failure model\"), a numeral, or one of the\nbounding phrases `at most`, `at least`, `up to`, `no more than`, `within`.\nOne failure lists every offending line with its word.\n**Answered by:** agent\n**Options:** same-line bound (chosen, as plan P7 of the source feature\nspecified); a bound anywhere in the criterion's block: rejected, it needs\nblock parsing the extractor does not have and makes the line in a diagnostic\nambiguous; a narrower word list without `any`: rejected, `any` is the word\nbehind most unbounded criteria, and D11 accepted the false positives.\n**Reversibility:** high; the list and phrases are one constant.\n**Decision:** same-line bound, D11 list, all offending lines reported.","file":"interview.md","heading":"D4. Unbounded-wording lint: scope, words and what counts as a bound"},{"answeredBy":"agent","body":"**Question:** D11 holds only specs validated under the new version; a resumed\nrun on an old spec gets a warning. How does `spec.validate` tell an old spec\nfrom a new one?\n**Answer:** Changing the parser already makes every earlier spec validation\nstale (the proof binds the parser's digest), so every run re-validates after\nan upgrade. An old spec is identified by its exact bytes, never by its run\nalone: a spec is grandfathered when its hash equals the spec of a validation\nthis run recorded (passed or failed) under an earlier planning policy, or of\na validation the run already grandfathered. A resumed run is the other case\nD11 names. `run.resume-current` with observed approvals of the unchanged\ncommitments (`planning.design` or `planning.implementation`) validates the\nspec as part of the resumption; that validation is grandfathered, because the\nsupplied approvals vouch that the spec on disk is the unchanged, earlier\napproved one. Its bytes then stay grandfathered. A spec validated in any other\nway without such a record (for example a spec rewritten after a resumption)\nis held to the rule. A grandfathered spec gets\nthe two checks as warnings that\nname the lines, and validation passes on the other facts; the proof records\n`grandfathered: true`. Any other spec, including a changed or rewritten spec\nin an old run, is held to the rule. The planning policy identifier moves from\n`planning-facts/1` to `planning-facts/2`. The portable `lint_spec.py` has no\nrun context and always applies the rule.\n**Answered by:** agent\n**Options:** (a) exact spec bytes already validated decide (chosen, after\ndesign review round 1 found that grandfathering a whole run lets a rewritten\nspec skip the rule, round 2 that a resumed run's first spec cannot be\nverified by bytes alone, and round 4 that D11 settles a warning for a resumed\nrun on an old spec; the resumption's own observed approvals supply that\nverification); (b) the whole run decides: rejected for that reason; (c)\nan opt-in marker in the spec: rejected, any new spec could skip the rule by\nleaving it out; (d) the run's start time against a release date: rejected,\nclocks and release dates are not protocol facts.\n**Reversibility:** medium; the rule for old specs is recorded in each proof,\nbut specs already validated under the new policy stay held to it.\n**Decision:** (a). An amendment of an old spec therefore adds a failure model,\nwhich is a small edit.","file":"interview.md","heading":"D5. Which specs are held to the new rule (versioning)"},{"answeredBy":"agent","body":"**Question:** What changes for a light brief?\n**Answer:** The brief may carry an optional `## Failure model` section,\ndocumented in `light-run.md`. There is no lint in a light run (light runs have\nno `spec.validate`). The brief-based reviewer instructions apply D2 to the\nbrief's section.\n**Answered by:** agent\n**Options:** none material; D14 settled it.\n**Reversibility:** high.\n**Decision:** as stated.","file":"interview.md","heading":"D6. Light runs"},{"answeredBy":"agent","body":"**Question:** How should light-run review judge documentation and operations\nprose, so it stops costing more rounds than the code it ships with?\n**Answer:** Brief-based reviewer instructions add a documentation bar. A\nfinding about documentation or operations prose (runbooks, guides, comments,\nREADME text) blocks only when a reader following the text as written, in a\nrealistic scenario, reaches a wrong or unsafe outcome: a wrong command, flag,\npath or value; a missing warning before a destructive or irreversible step; a\nstatement that contradicts the shipped code or the brief. Wording, style,\ntone, structure, narrower restatements of an already fixed point, rare edge\ncases the text does not mention, and suggestions to document more are low.\nThe bar never lowers the brief: documentation that the brief's goal or scope\nasks for is a requirement, and a missing or wrong part of it is judged like\nany other unmet requirement (design review round 1).\n**Answered by:** agent\n**Options:** (a) a reviewer instruction bar for light runs (chosen); (b)\nmechanically demote findings on `*.md` paths: rejected, it changes the\nreviewer's meaning (correction policy) and misses prose in code comments; (c)\none reviewer instead of two for docs-only light changes: rejected, it removes\na required acceptance gate, which is a protocol change far beyond #143; (d)\napply the bar in full runs too: not chosen, the issue asks for light mode,\nand full-rigor specs can state their own documentation criteria. Trent may\nwant (d) later.\n**Reversibility:** high; instruction text.\n**Decision:** (a), light runs only.","file":"interview.md","heading":"D7. A \"good enough\" bar for documentation in light runs"},{"answeredBy":"agent","body":"**Question:** Changing the reviewer instructions changes the review contract\nfingerprint. Should the review contract version move?\n**Answer:** Yes: `REVIEW_CONTRACT_VERSION` moves to a new value, so results\nretained by runs in flight keep their recorded qualification on resume (the\nexisting AC11 behaviour), rather than every in-flight run losing its review\nevidence on upgrade. Within the new version the fingerprint must match\nexactly, as before. The planning review contract has no version constant; its\nrecorded reviews carry their own contract identity.\n**Answered by:** agent\n**Options:** bump (chosen); keep the version, so in-flight reviews no longer\nqualify after upgrade: rejected, it forces re-review for an instruction-only\nchange.\n**Reversibility:** high. A merge conflict with #142 on the same constant is\nexpected and trivial.\n**Decision:** bump.","file":"interview.md","heading":"D8. Versioning of the reviewer instructions"},{"answeredBy":"agent","body":"**Question:** Is any of the three items much larger than the others (the\nmandate's split rule)?\n**Answer:** No. Calibration and the docs bar are instruction text with tests;\nAC8 is a parser extension, one state field, template and procedure updates.\nAll three ship in this PR.\n**Answered by:** agent\n**Options:** ship all three (chosen); split AC8 out: not needed.\n**Reversibility:** n/a.\n**Decision:** ship all three.","file":"interview.md","heading":"D9. Scope size check"},{"answeredBy":"agent","body":"- **Answered by:** agent\n- **Decision:** The design planning review (Codex gpt-6-sol, high) failed in\n  rounds 1-5. Rounds 1-3 found real gaps, all fixed (whole-run grandfathering,\n  docs bar lowering brief requirements, inconsistent wording). Rounds 2, 4 and\n  5 then disagreed with each other on resumed runs: round 2 objected to\n  grandfathering an unverifiable resumed spec, round 4 required the warning\n  the source D11 settled for a resumed run on an old spec, and round 5\n  objected to the resulting exemption. Round 6 would hand back (park). The\n  design was approved under `autonomous-evaluator` authority with a recorded\n  judgment: round 5's finding is accepted residual risk (it needs a\n  resumption approval that misstates what the human approved), and the spec\n  narrows it further (S3).\n- **Alternatives:** park at round 6 for Trent (rejected: the open question is\n  settled input from D11, not a new defect); drop the resumed-run warning\n  (rejected: contradicts D11, round 4).\n- **Reversibility:** high; the resumed-run rule is one condition in\n  `core.js`.","file":"scratchpad.md","heading":"S2. Design approved by autonomous judgment at planning round 5"},{"answeredBy":"agent","body":"- **Answered by:** agent\n- **Decision:** `run.resume-current` grandfathers the spec it validates only\n  when the supplied `planning.implementation` approval names the current\n  commitments, whose identity includes the exact spec bytes. A design-only\n  resumption does not grandfather (round 5's concrete hole: a design approval\n  does not bind the spec).\n- **Alternatives:** any resumption approval grandfathers (rejected: a design\n  approval does not cover spec bytes); no resumption grandfathers (rejected:\n  D11).\n- **Reversibility:** high.","file":"scratchpad.md","heading":"S3. Only an implementation approval grandfathers a spec on resumption"},{"answeredBy":"agent","body":"- **Answered by:** agent\n- **Decision:** The numeral bound ignores requirement identifiers (`AC1`,\n  `R2`, `I-3`, `D4`, `P5`); otherwise every AC heading and rubric row would\n  carry a numeral and pass the lint. Other numbers (counts, values such as\n  `42`) count as a bound, as D4 decided.\n- **Alternatives:** count only numbers next to a unit or comparator\n  (rejected: guesswork the lint cannot get right).\n- **Reversibility:** high; one regular expression.","file":"scratchpad.md","heading":"S4. Requirement identifiers are not numeral bounds"},{"answeredBy":"agent","body":"- **Answered by:** agent\n- **Decision:** `execution.js` exports `evaluatorInstructions` and\n  `delegation.js` exports `planningInstructions`, so tests assert the exact\n  text each reviewer receives. No other change to those modules beyond the\n  instruction text and `REVIEW_CONTRACT_VERSION`.\n- **Alternatives:** assert through a fake reviewer request only (kept for the\n  light path in `current-light.test.js`; the unit test is more direct).\n- **Reversibility:** high.","file":"scratchpad.md","heading":"S5. Instruction builders exported for tests"}],"rule":"This is a goal run: the human was not asked. Assess every supplied agent-answered decision entry against the fixed mandate and the approved requirements. An entry that contradicts the mandate, exceeds its scope or makes an irreversible choice the human would need to make is blocking."},"artifact":"spec","contract":"gatereeve/current/8","documents":{"design.md":{"bytes":7123,"hash":"sha256:489d5f888b1cb8cb4f2c212529c78bb6279c512842d60f30e14cf81375b5158d","path":"design.md"},"issues.md":{"bytes":1033,"hash":"sha256:46ed7748deb61735df4217381892e8f1537e61c0ad57b31a2ff2f409f736852b","path":"issues.md"},"plan.md":{"bytes":2501,"hash":"sha256:11b54d239b2894a558be96d46370c130b640d6d77ca41ed096c06c24741aad12","path":"plan.md"},"spec.md":{"bytes":10988,"hash":"sha256:32da849ccda27c38abbb0f5d3cd533d8de97b8bc736f4da5d4a061c6a91adbea","path":"spec.md"}},"evaluator":{"contextId":"01a21bd5-38d3-7050-a11a-35e33d1a6d7f","freshContext":true,"inheritedTurns":0,"model":"gpt-6-sol","provider":"codex","reasoningEffort":"high"},"featureId":"tb-convergence-severity-calibration","findings":[{"blocking":true,"classId":"acceptance-design-conflict","classStatement":"An acceptance criterion must preserve the approved design’s severity rule.","document":"requirements/spec.md","evidence":"AC1, lines 50–55, calls a scenario realistic if it arises in ordinary use or under a handled condition. The design, lines 34–38, requires ordinary use within handled conditions with no one acting unusually. The supplied D1 decision also says an unusual scenario stays low regardless of consequence.","failureScenario":"A handled provider outage is uncommon but has a severe consequence -> the spec’s “or” permits a high rating where the approved severity rule calls for low, causing an avoidable blocking review finding.","id":"F1","severity":"medium"},{"blocking":true,"classId":"unreconciled-design-change","classStatement":"A later acceptance criterion cannot silently reverse an approved compatibility rule.","document":"requirements/spec.md","evidence":"AC5, lines 101–104, says a design-only resumption does not grandfather a spec. The approved design, lines 61–67, says resumption with observed planning.design or planning.implementation approval receives warnings. The supplied S3 decision narrows this after the design without reconciling its text.","failureScenario":"An unchanged old spec is resumed with only an observed design approval -> implementation following AC5 fails validation while the approved design requires warnings, interrupting the resumed run.","id":"F2","severity":"medium"},{"blocking":true,"classId":"ambiguous-lint-bound","classStatement":"The lint criterion must distinguish a numeric bound from a requirement identifier.","document":"requirements/spec.md","evidence":"AC4, lines 86–94, allows any same-line numeral to satisfy the unbounded-wording lint; R4, line 155, calls this a same-line bound. The supplied S4 decision explicitly excludes requirement identifiers such as AC1 and R2 from numeral bounds, but neither AC4 nor R4 states that exception.","failureScenario":"An author writes “AC1. Always succeeds” -> counting the identifier’s numeral makes spec.validate pass an unbounded criterion and report success despite AC4’s purpose.","id":"F3","severity":"high"},{"blocking":true,"classId":"rubric-work-uncovered","classStatement":"The plan and issues must cover every required R5 compatibility path and artifact.","document":"requirements/plan.md","evidence":"P2–P3, lines 31–39, cover earlier recorded spec validations and returning warnings, but name neither the implementation-approval path through run.resume-current nor retaining warnings in the proof. Both are required by AC5, lines 101–112, and R5, line 156; issues.md I-2 only repeats the broad P2–P3 assignment.","failureScenario":"The implementer completes P2 and P3 as written -> a resumed spec is not grandfathered through its implementation approval, or its warnings are absent from the retained proof, so R5 fails.","id":"F4","severity":"medium"},{"blocking":true,"classId":"mandated-deliverable-omitted","classStatement":"The implementation plan must include the fixed PR deliverable and its required closing text.","document":"requirements/plan.md","evidence":"The strategy says “One PR” at lines 15–21, but steps P1–P5 and the final step at lines 23–52 end with checks and a completion report. issues.md I-1–I-3 likewise contain no PR delivery task. The fixed goal requires a ready, unmerged PR whose body says “Closes #143.”","failureScenario":"All listed plan steps and issue tasks finish -> the work is reported complete without creating the required ready PR or putting “Closes #143” in its body.","id":"F5","severity":"high"}],"kind":"planning-review","mandate":{"constraints":["The issue and docs/issues/tb-convergence-and-retirement/follow-up-feature.md are the design input.","GateReeve corrects a verdict automatically only where the answer follows from the verdict itself; meaning-changing gaps go to the repair turn.","Keep edits to reviewer instructions, spec templates and lint, and light-mode docs review; keep any edit to files owned by #142 (execution.js, boundary-review.js, local-agent-adapters.js, reviewer packet assembly) or #144 (sweep records, round-policy dispositions) small.","If one of the three scope items turns out much larger than the others, deliver the other two and leave it open with a comment on #143, rather than growing the PR.","Where a choice is genuinely open, decide it on the merits, record it in the decision log, and call it out in the PR description for Trent to check.","Don't put local paths or other projects' names in public issue or PR text.","Don't merge."],"goal":"One PR that closes GitHub issue #143 (severity calibration, failure model in specs (AC8), and a good-enough bar for docs in light mode), ready and unmerged, with \"Closes #143\" in the PR body."},"mandateAssessment":{"evidence":"The proposed work stays within issue #143’s three scope items. The plan calls for one PR, limits changes to execution.js to instructions and the contract version, and does not propose merging or changing verdict schemas or reviewer packet assembly. No supplied agent decision exceeds the fixed mandate. The plan’s missing PR delivery steps are a separate blocking finding.","outcome":"PASS"},"outcome":"FAIL","output":{"bytes":4385,"format":"json","hash":"sha256:7e8b06391447121a41c27cb921d4d2f37c506f661e7d3d3be67eb81257ace784"},"receipt":{"bytes":1369,"format":"json","hash":"sha256:c5a718bb779d9003c4719b40a28f58e6961cefb0bda372157b1d1d4834f8cad7"},"reviewContract":"sha256:ead3f9c92af273e529c77b97d253cb77e89ce43e589246dc767b07f0ecee9bab","runId":"tb-convergence-severity-calibration-start-1","subject":"sha256:6bddc60ccc43060720fd23cb2908f369dcd8e41511c3f6816a8a3149ffc3768c","summary":"Implementation should not be authorized yet. The spec conflicts with the design on severity and old-run behavior, leaves a central lint exception ambiguous, and the plan omits required compatibility and PR delivery work."}